Raminder (also adding Alicia and Anna),
&nbsp;
Below are our thoughts on a response to Michael Burke’s most recent emails.&nbsp; This is
 all subject to input from Clydes, who we understand you are speaking with. We would be happy to coordinate with them.


We think that we should present to Michael Burke two options.&nbsp; Either: (i) continue to work on the case at PG until the end of his notice period, which
 would be in the best interests of the client; or (ii), go on gardening leave.&nbsp; PG will not, however, be releasing him from his notice period and PG will be enforcing his covenants and will take action against both him and anyone who tries to employ him ahead
 of the end of his notice period.&nbsp; If he stays at PG, he must stop with his tactics to exit early and focus on his job.
This puts the ball firmly in his court to choose between acting in the best interests of his clients or for his own personal reasons.&nbsp; It is a reasonable
 response that can then be justified to LD and others as necessary: he had the option to act in the best interests of the client by staying on and working, but if he goes on gardening leave that was his choice for his own reasons.
A draft from Raminder along those lines is below.&nbsp; Again, we say that this should be
 sent to Clyde &amp; Co for their input.
We assume you are doing so already, but it would be sensible to have contingency plans
 for both external and internal comms should any article be published by The Times.
Separately, please could we be sent a copy of the internal report into Tom Goodhead
 and/or the other unnamed senior male staff member?&nbsp; We had been told previously that we would be sent a copy and have not yet.&nbsp; It would be helpful for our own purposes and so we can advise accordingly.
In addition, Burke refers to “email chains which confirm Gramercy, as recently as
 September, were directly involved in the NOX Litigation and they have been giving direct input into Litigations Management Agreements and Agency Agreements. Yesterday, Anna Varga instructed a member of staff to engage with the company retained by Gramercy
 to give direct input into these documents - despite assurances given to me and the staff directly by the Board and Anna that Gramercy are not involved in the Litigation”. We have no idea what he is referring to ion these two instances. Can you inform us?
&nbsp;
--- FROM RAMINDER ---
&nbsp;
Hi Mike,
Thank you for your email.&nbsp; I have also been provided with your email to Alicia from
 today regarding a potential article in The Times and suggestions of criminal conduct.&nbsp; This email responds to them both.
As previously communicated to you, we will not be releasing you from your notice period.&nbsp;
 Your continued attempts to leave earlier than your contract allows are however proving disruptive to our efforts to safeguard good governance within the firm.&nbsp; We are, therefore, minded to put you on gardening leave for the remainder of your notice period.&nbsp;
 We would view that as regrettable since we are in the midst of the PDD Trial and would much prefer to have you remain on the team.&nbsp; However, your continued conduct threatens to seriously damage morale and be more disruptive than helpful, taking account of
 the best interests of our clients.
We therefore give you the choice of either (i) stay working throughout the remainder
 of your notice period or (ii) go on gardening leave effective immediately.
If you continue to try to exit early, we can only conclude that you are grasping for
 reasons to engineer such an exit for your own reasons, which is unprofessional, threatens breaches of your employment contract, and is not in the best interests of our clients.&nbsp; We put you on notice that we will not hesitate to take legal action against you
 to enforce your employment contract and all covenants, nor will we hesitate to take action against any firm that might seek to employ you ahead of your notice period expiring or in breach of your covenants.
If you choose to remain at the firm, we must insist that you focus solely on your work
 and only raise any further concerns with sufficient particularity and justification so that we can understand them fully and investigate them if necessary.&nbsp; Unclear and unparticularised allegations as you have been raising for the past several weeks are simply
 improper and must stop.
Regarding the specific points raised in your emails:
&nbsp;


[Gramercy allegations – who/what is he speaking about?]
Your suggestion that you cannot act in the best interests of our clients under supervision from other staff is not understood.&nbsp; If you continue to believe
 this is the case, please explain which members of staff and why, by precise reference to specific provisions of your contract or the SRA Code of Conduct, say this is so.
You refer vaguely to potential criminal conduct.&nbsp; This is obviously a very serious allegation.&nbsp; Please now state clearly and unequivocally what precise
 conduct you are referring to and by which specific people so that we can investigate immediately.
As you are aware, the firm has already taken, and continues to take, robust action against Tom Goodhead.&nbsp; We take our obligations to our stakeholders
 extremely seriously and there is not, therefore, any basis for you to seek to exit early.&nbsp; Even if there has been improper behaviour at the Firm – and rest assured, we will investigate any such allegations seriously – that does not give you any justification,
 under either your contract or your regulatory obligations. Conjecture about what may or may not be published in
The Times does not change that position.
I look forward to hearing back from you regarding going onto gardening leave, although
 we remain hopeful that you will agree to continue to work throughout the PDD Trial.
All of the firm’s rights are fully reserved.
&nbsp;
Kind regards,
&nbsp;
